# Runtime management

Mode: Operate. Heath requested a configuration and management interface, not
more read-only summaries. Preserve the existing read-only dashboard as a separate
access boundary. This page is the private, single-operator control surface.

Use the existing cobalt, white, cool-paper, ink, and amber identity. Keep native
controls, readable tables/records, visible keyboard focus, and mobile navigation.
No new frontend framework, downloaded assets, or separate frontend server.

Primary journey: unlock → inspect → edit a draft → validate → compare with active
configuration → save → activate in shadow → inspect new events → recover through
a prior revision. The page must distinguish saved, active, pending, and failed
states. Event text is always text, never HTML. Search URLs may contain private
terms. Lock removes private editor/evidence content and clears the in-memory token.

Overview, Activity, Review, Configure, and Runtime are real page areas. Advanced
configuration uses YAML; model/source/basic-policy forms edit the same draft. No operating-system
service controls, browser secret enrollment, live promotion, or invented model
health. Reviews change labels only. Evaluation calls no adapters.

## Guided service monitor

Configure now leads with one task: connect a decision model → select exact system
units → review, test, and apply in shadow. Keep the established Operate palette
and native controls. Show one step at a time; place scope and test evidence beside
each other on desktop and in reading order on phones. YAML and the original
forms belong under Advanced configuration, not beside first-run fields.

The guide creates matching questions and provider-compatible policy. It must not
silently rewrite multi-source/filter/agent/override pipelines or replace custom
Advanced edits merely because someone opens the guide. Existing monitor names
and paused states remain intact. Test results are for the exact editor text and
are cleared on edits, reload, or lock. Tests make explicit, bounded synthetic model
calls, not source reads or notifications. Missing credentials and example models
must not appear ready. Secrets remain outside the browser; no deployment is part
of this UI change.

## Verification

Synthetic Chrome tests at 390px and 1440px checked navigation, horizontal overflow,
login, forms, draft/diff/activation, rollback, event inspection and Back navigation,
an exact large JSON identity, hostile text, review, and locking during a delayed
response body. Guided tests also cover placeholder models, missing credentials,
explicit inference consent, edit-invalidated results, provider-specific policy,
paused-source identity, and preservation of a custom Advanced draft. Tests use a temporary SQLite database and local
HTTP handlers; no real model, credentials, or remote runtime is required. Optional
browser runner: `MRMR_CHROME=<path> go test ./cmd/mrmr -run TestManagementBrowser`.
Desktop and phone screenshots were inspected. The final phone navigation uses
one row at 390px with 44px targets. No deployment was performed.

The design detector reported advisory differences from the root offline-wiki
DESIGN.md: 13/14/20/24/32px type steps and restrained danger/warning colors (#9a292d,
#854011). These are intentional control-surface choices. Its first HTML pass
could not resolve the absolute asset route; assets now use relative URLs and
real-browser checks verify their styles. No detector findings were suppressed.
